From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nfqueue: retire scheduled token cleanups together, in the
 pump's turn

A packet honk sheds before its flow reaches the correlator (the ingest
queue full, the backend not readable within the hold) schedules a token
cleanup that retires its kernel staging, which otherwise lasts until
120 s after the flow's first packet, every packet of the flow queued and
dropped meanwhile. The watchdog drained the cleanups each tick one at a
time, each in a fenced retirement batch of one, which waits for a grace
period of its own, and gave up until the next tick whenever the backend
was held. The set is bounded by the queue's length, and reaching the
bound is fatal ("scheduled cleanup set reached NFQUEUE maxlen"). On an
emulated NanoPi R4S under host load, 200 new UDP flows a second that
wait for honk's decision were shed at some 100 a second, a retirement
took 80 ms, and the drain found the backend free for six of them in the
33 s the set took to fill; honk then exited, and its shutdown spent five
more minutes retiring the 4096 one by one.

Retire a scheduled token cleanup together with the other token
cleanups scheduled, up to a retirement batch, under one fence and one
grace period. That was not enough alone: the drain still found no turn
in nearly every tick, the ingest actor taking the backend for reading
again as soon as it let it go. And a drain that waits its turn may hold
the backend for a whole batch (seconds there) while an armed flow waits
for its activation, which misses its hold: honk exited that way too. So
while the commit pump has work, it retires the token cleanups itself, a
batch after each of its own batches, when none of its flows is armed,
waiting its turn for the backend as it does for its commits. The
watchdog retires them while the pump is idle, still without waiting,
and now waits for no other drainer either; it retires a batch a tick,
so its pause before it scans the holds is one grace period at most. A
flow cleanup still settles its cell, a refusal is still recorded per
request and a failure is still fatal. retire_pending takes a slice of
requests, and ebpf::retire_pending, its single-request wrapper, goes.

Tests: a retirement batch and one more of scheduled token cleanups are
retired in two backend batches (one at a time: a batch each), every
staged entry gone and the set empty, and by the watchdog in two ticks,
a batch each; the pump retires a cleanup after its batch within a
second while a reader takes the backend in turn (without the pump's
retirement: none in that time); and a late request of a batch is never
applied, its staging left to its token cleanup. The design document
describes the drain.

Signed-off-by: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
